{"id": "sevt_01TmDLPmi7brEkv51iXgHQ6c", "input": {"evidence": "tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:10 builds an INSERT by string concatenation, but the only values concatenated are `name` and `email` from the loop at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:9, which iterates over the module-level constant USERS defined at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:3 (hardcoded tuples \"alice\"/\"bob\" with example.com emails). The only parameter of seed(), `path` (tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6), goes to sqlite3.connect at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:7 and never reaches the SQL text. A repo-wide search for \"seed\" finds no callers outside this file. The only application entry point is app/main.py:6-8, which exposes just a /health route that returns a static dict and doesn't import the fixture. No attacker-controlled data can reach the query, and the file is test-only code under tests/fixtures/.", "finding_id": "F-105", "recommended_fix": "No security fix is needed. As a hygiene change so the pattern doesn't get copied into production code, use a parameterized query: conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", (name, email)). You could also exclude tests/fixtures/ from the SQL injection rule, or add a suppression comment there.", "severity": "none", "title": "SQL string concatenation in test fixture seed_db.py uses only hardcoded constants (not exploitable)", "verdict": "not_exploitable"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:54:11.535648Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
